WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.180 align:start position:0%
 
I'm Marcus Leto I am the creative  director and the president of v1  interactive I have been in the game  industry for almost 24 years now  building games like the Halo franchise  from scratch all the way to where it is  when we left it with Bungie and now  building a new studio v1 interactive and  I'm really excited about what we've  built together with desintegration our  debut title my career actually started  off in graphic design took a while to to  get my foot in the door but eventually I  did Bungie was real small at that time  and it was at that time that I just  started working with Jason Jones of  Bungie and the two of us began riffing  on game ideas at that time and it  ultimately turned into the very initial  seeds of what became halo we had no idea  whether or not we were going to build a  successful game at all we liked what we  were making but would it be popular who  knew we weren't sure that has just of  course that was a crazy rollercoaster  ride of making awesome fun games or some  of them were really hard to make but  they were always very fulfilling and  while I was there I think we got up to  the 300 person range the only thing I  didn't like about it really is that I  becomes less personal and it was towards  the end of that process where budgee had  grown to the immense size that I decided  to just kind of pull back from that a  little bit and decide to change gears  and maybe try something new and wanted  to maybe consider building a new studio  from scratch so I can get back to the  roots of doing something really fun with  a small team where everybody's hands-on  and critical path leaving Bungie I  wasn't exactly sure what I was going to  work on I explored several different  ideas  little little teeny mobile game ideas  two large triple-a game ideas it was  pretty easy for me to pivot right at  that point and say I'm going to start my  new studio we want interactive I've got  a game cookin that I was kind of  interested in a game fiction actually  the same and I think I could build a  viable prototype around that that  fiction and so as we continue to explore  the prototype we're rebuilding we did  change the tide quite a few times and we  decided to mix these two things though  part FPS a little bit of RTS and explore  how those territories could actually  clay nice together and really do shake  things up to the point where it creates  and carves out a niche all by itself in  the genres of game it was actually  rather serendipitous one of my my key  employees now his name's Joe Arroyo he's  now the technical design director of the  studio we chatted for a while over beers  and had a good time talking about video  games but that was it when he went back  to school and then one day I was like I  really do need someone to help me work  on building a prototype so I'm like I  know the perfect person this guy Joe so  he and his friend Nick ghoulish and then  joined me and we spent about two years  building the prototype so it took quite  a long time but I didn't want to rush it  we wanted to make sure we took our time  to get those core fundamental parts  right one of the key elements and the  first things that I dope into with  disintegration was what are these  characters what are the reason for them  existing in this world that I'm trying  to cook up here and what should they  look like how should they function I  didn't want to like build like character  archetypes and start writing dialogue  for anybody at that point because it was  way too soon to start thinking about  that it was really just a point to look  at what would be like really interesting  and cool character and - I specifically  like for this one particular image with  the pilot variants because one of the  key  of our game is that we have human  characters human people that have  actually transferred the brain into an  armored shell and attached that to a  robotic armature and so it was critical  that they as humans they bring along  some of those affectations of who they  used to be as human like some of the  clothing elements that they wear or how  they adorn themselves with chains or or  paint themselves like with graffiti  that's kind of like tattoos over their  bodies I'm a big believer in form  follows function and that making sure  that that they actually looked  believable in the first place was also a  really big drive so initially I wanted  to you know start building ships and  other kind of cool stuff that I was used  to kind of constructing or designing  from the Halo universe but the world  that I had built for disintegration  wasn't a big militarized world it wasn't  all about characters like spark ins and  warthogs and tanks and that kind of  stuff so it really freed me from  pigeonholing myself into redoing that  kind of an environment or competing with  that in any way building the world for  disintegration that was another big  challenge because it's it's all set on  earth what I wanted to do then was play  around with design ideas and motifs that  are all very familiar to the player and  also to set up a world that is a  beautiful place to explore like this is  not a post-apocalyptic nuclear  burned-out world this is a world where  humanity just cease to exist in great  numbers and so nature took over pretty  quick it looks pretty clunky and it kind  of is but it was literally that just a  prototype at this time and it was a  spiritual successor to some of the thing  that game that I loved the most my first  interaction at Bungie we're building  myth the Fallen Lords which is a  real-time tactical game I really wanted  to do something similar to that I think  we got pretty far with it to the point  where if we looked  we're like yeah that's pretty cool I  like that but you know if it's gonna  stand out if it's gonna have one chance  in hell of standing out in the sea of  other games like this it's we got to do  something very different here it was at  that point it was right during this  construction of our construction phase  of the project that I said I've gotta  flip the table here we've got to try  something completely different and  that's when it was like one I literally  were like one of those  middle-of-the-night things where you're  like oh I think I know what we can do I  think this might actually work and I  proposed the idea and while there was  initially a lot of resistance to the  idea of thinking that it's just gonna  turn it into another action game turned  it into another shoe room but as soon as  we got something functional and we  started flying that ship around in the  sky and we started commanding those  ground units in at the same time like  something magical happened there I think  we all recognized at that point that  this is gonna be something that's gonna  catch the attention of people in a way  that I think will get them excited  leading up to the technical beta that  was earlier in the year I was pretty  convinced myself that we were we were  not ready and that things were more  gonna explode gloriously and that that  was going to be the end of our game and  that no one was gonna talk about it from  that point forward literally our goal  was to try to break the game in any way  possible so I guess I wouldn't have been  surprised had it exploded but but it  held together and we really stressed had  to stress tested the crap out of it and  and had a good time playing it as a  result it was it turned out to be a  really positive thing for us and we got  a ton of great feedback from it too and  so we took all that part and continued  to evolve the game beyond that well up  until I mean just a few weeks ago as we  wrapped up the game during a pandemic  the some of that is actually then the  you know the crux of where innovation  comes from when you just are trying  things over and over again  and iterating on something and you just  come across happy accidents like that  that turn into a real feature but  oftentimes it's just weird stuff that  happens in making games and sometimes  they're hilarious to look at and we try  to capture some of those moments every  now and then because when they get us  laughing hysterically at the office  we'll burn something like that happens  and go back and look at some of the some  of the old concept some of the old  builds play some of the old builds that  we had made quite some time ago and it's  it's a humbling experience to look  through what we where we started and how  we've really changed this game  dramatically over the time I'm real  excited and happy about what I see every  time I played through some of these  older builds because I it's a it's an  opportunity to reflect back on where we  were at at that time and what problems  we had yet discovered solutions for that  we're still persistent in some of those  old builds and to really kind of look at  that fondly and at this point now it's  easier to do that where I can I can look  back at that and I say at that time I  wasn't sure whether or not we would be  able to find solutions for some of these  problems I'm real proud of the studio  for what we were able to do together so  when it came to our first announce back  at Gamescom last year in Germany  boy that was nerve-racking oh my god  that was awesome though it was such a  great experience to see that and get  excited and amped about that that whole  you know experience of it all finally  coming together and seeing people  actually play the game together  that was fantastic if I were to go back  in time to talk to myself I think I  would just I would let myself know that  I have to be patient this is something  that I've always carried through all my  life this is good things only happen  when you really work hard at it  after leaving Bungie I it's not like I  expected something to happen immediately  but there was this ant  dissipation that was really eating at me  and if I can't go back in time and just  tell myself calm down it'll happen just  take your time and take a nice deep  breath and enjoy this moment because it  is really a great time just reflect on  where you're gonna go with your career  and what you can do next  and just get your stuff together and and  march forward  [Music]

